{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Send for yourself men.</b> Because Israel said (Deuteronomy 1:22), ״נִשְׁלְחָה לְפָנֵינוּ אֲנָשִׁים וְיַחְפְּרוּ לָנוּ אֶת הָאָרֶץ״ — ״לָנוּ״ means for our pleasure and our benefit, and the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses, ״Send for yourself״ and not for them — for for you specifically the mission would be for pleasure and for good, but for them it would not be for their benefit, for through this mission death was decreed upon them, while for Moses it caused that he would live another forty years. For it had already been decreed upon Moses that he would not see [the fulfillment of] what was done for the seven kings of the seven nations, and through the sin of the spies he was delayed forty years." "Another thing: ״Send for yourself men״ — you specifically shall see by the holy spirit whether the men are fit for this mission, for most of the world errs regarding flatterers who present themselves as fit and don a cloak of hair for the sake of falsehood. Therefore He said ״for yourself men״ — specifically those who are men in your eyes, important men, and not those who are men in the eyes of others, for it is possible that their inside is not like their outside. Another thing: ״for yourself men״ — to you they appear to be important men, for you have eyes of flesh to see only in the revealed realm as it stands at present; at that moment they were fit. But in My eyes they are not fit men, for I see with two visions — in the present and in the future — for they are destined to be in an evil counsel." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 16:6) that the juxtaposition of the spies to Miriam is because Miriam was stricken on account of the matter of evil speech, and these wicked men saw yet did not take instruction. And their intent is to say that immediately after the episode of Miriam the spies were sent, for the Holy One, blessed be He, said: lest each one of them relent and take instruction upon seeing what happened to Miriam. And for this reason it specifies here ״men,״ because it says (Numbers 12:1), ״וַתְּדַבֵּר מִרְיָם וְאַהֲרֹן בְּמֹשֶׁה״, and it should have said ״וַיְדַבְּרוּ״, for ״וַתְּדַבֵּר״ refers back to Miriam — and furthermore, we find no punishment for Aaron; rather, Scripture informs you that evil speech is found more among women than among men, for ten measures of speech descended to the world, nine were taken by women, and so on (Kiddushin 49b). And since ordinary women are prattling talkers, therefore it suspended the speech on Miriam, for she began this corruption and Aaron was secondary to her. Therefore He said: ״Send for yourself men״ — who do not have the way of women, and who will not be like Miriam, who told evil speech; but rather men in truth, whose way is not to tell evil speech." "Another thing: therefore He specified ״men,״ because our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yalkut Shimoni, Pinchas 927) — the men were haters of the land and said, ״נִתְּנָה רֹאשׁ וְנָשׁוּבָה מִצְרָיְמָה״ (Numbers 14:4), and the women were lovers of the land and said, ״תְּנָה לָּנוּ אֲחֻזָּה״ (ibid. 27:4). And therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, said: according to My knowledge, seeing as I see the future, it would have been better to send women, who love the land, for they would not speak ill of it; but according to your knowledge — you who think that they are fit and you who think that the land is beloved to them — you shall send men. And this is ״Send for yourself״ — according to your knowledge, ״men״; but according to My knowledge it would have been better to send women, as stated." "<b>And they shall tour the land of Canaan.</b> We find three expressions concerning this matter: וְיָתֻרוּ, and וְיַחְפְּרוּ, and וַיְרַגְּלוּ. For Israel said (Deuteronomy 1:22): ״נִשְׁלְחָה אֲנָשִׁים לְפָנֵינוּ וְיַחְפְּרוּ לָנוּ אֶת הָאָרֶץ״. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said: ״וְיָתֻרוּ אֶת אֶרֶץ כְּנַעַן״. And concerning the spies it is written (ibid. 1:24): ״וַיְרַגְּלוּ אוֹתָהּ״. Because there were three opinions in that counsel: the Holy One, blessed be He, said ״וְיָתֻרוּ״, which carries also the meaning of superiority (יִתְרוֹן), for the Holy One, blessed be He, wanted to show them the superiority of the land over all lands, as it is written (Exodus 3:8): ״וּלְהַעֲלֹתוֹ מִן הָאָרֶץ הַהִיא אֶל אֶרֶץ טוֹבָה וּרְחָבָה״ — and this ״good״ is the spiritual good, on account of its being an air that makes wise (Bava Batra 158b), for this prepares a person to arrive through it to the category of true good, for man has no advantage (יִתְרוֹן) under the sun besides this, and the advantage of this land is in everything (Ecclesiastes 5:8). But Israel had a different spirit with them, for they were always seeking a pretext to return to Egypt, and therefore they said ״וְיַחְפְּרוּ״, which carries the meaning of disgrace (חֶרְפָּה), for they wanted to see the nakedness (עֶרְוַת) of the land so that they would have grounds to lodge and say, ״נִתְּנָה רֹאשׁ וְנָשׁוּבָה מִצְרָיְמָה״. But in any case they did not ask to fabricate from their own hearts a lying report. And the spies corrupted and worsened beyond what was asked of them, and fabricated from their own hearts false things, in the manner of every talebearer who exaggerates every matter to tell more than what he saw; therefore it says ״וַיְרַגְּלוּ אוֹתָהּ״. For רֶגֶל means רָכִיל (talebearer), as Rashi explained on the verse לֹא תֵלֵךְ רָכִיל (Leviticus 19:16)." "One may also explain why the Holy One, blessed be He, said ״וְיָתוּרוּ״ — for in truth that land is the head of all lands, by virtue of several excellent qualities redoubled, which it possesses in surpassing elevation above all the rest of the earth. And so it is brought in the Yalkut on this pericope (13, §223) concerning the land of Israel, the verse ״וְרֹאשׁ עַפְרוֹת תֵּבֵל״ (Proverbs 8:26). But they did the opposite of this, saying that it is inferior to all lands, for it is a land that devours its inhabitants, and they brought it down from the rank of the head to the rank of the foot. Therefore it says ״וַיְרַגְּלוּ אוֹתָהּ״ — that they made it into a footstool, like this foot which is the lowest part of the whole body. But in truth it is the head of all lands, for on this account, in every instance, the language of ascent is used for one who goes up to the land of Israel. And concerning this they said: ״נִתְּנָה רֹאשׁ וְנָשׁוּבָה מִצְרָיְמָה״ — we find that נְתִינָה is the language of relinquishing, as in ״לֹא יִתֵּן אֶתְכֶם מֶלֶךְ מִצְרַיִם לַהֲלֹךְ״ (Exodus 3:19), whose Targum is ״לֹא יִשְׁבּוֹק״. Thus they said here: let us relinquish the head — that land which you say is the head of all lands — וְנָשׁוּבָה מִצְרָיְמָה, even though they say that it is below the rank of the land of Israel." "And as for what is written with the language of מְרַגְּלִים regarding those whom Joshua sent — even though they were righteous, Scripture nevertheless calls them מְרַגְּלִים, because they went to make all the people who dwell in it into a footstep for Israel, or to make a pathway for Israel in the land. But here it says ״וַיְרַגְּלוּ אוֹתָהּ״, which implies that they made the land itself into the rank of a foot. And as for what is written concerning Joshua, ״כִּי לַחְפֹּר אֶת כָּל הָאָרֶץ בָּאוּ״ (Joshua 2:3) — because they made themselves out to be sellers of pots, as it says (ibid. 2:1) ״חֶרֶשׁ לֵאמֹר״, and the way of potters is to dig holes and pits in the earth, and that place was the dung-heap of Jericho, an important place. Therefore they said about them: see, they have come לַחְפֹּר אֶת הָאָרֶץ — to dig holes in the earth in order to make themselves pots, and thereby they would ruin the land."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {5 "The Yalkut citation is given as 'יג תשמג' in the source; I render it as pericope 13, section 223."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:26.230671944Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}